WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.490
With the rise of the Islamic state, or Daesh,
back in 2014 we witnessed the terrible

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.809
and awful crimes that they committed
against the religious minorities in the

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:14.400
region. And some of the worst of these
crimes were committed against the Yazidi

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.779
community, particularly in the Sinjar
Mountain region, which garnered a lot of

00:00:18.779 --> 00:00:22.769
international attention of course. But
this also led a lot of people to ask the

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:28.240
question: "Who are the Yazidis?" and "What is the Yazidi religion"?

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.800
The Yazidis are referred to as an ethno-religious
community, and one that is specifically

00:00:36.809 --> 00:00:41.910
connected to the Kurdish people of
northern Iraq in regions like Sheikhan,

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:47.360
the Sinjar mountains and in the region
of Lalish. Now, what we mean by

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.770
ethno-religious community is that the
religion and ethnicity is so strongly

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:57.239
interconnected that we cannot really
separate one from the other. This is

00:00:57.239 --> 00:01:02.010
shown by the fact that your
religious identity as a Yazidi is primarily

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:06.510
determined by your parents being Yazidis
and that the religion doesn't accept any

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:11.549
converts. We usually understand religion
from a very modern and often Christian-

00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:16.290
centric framework in which religion is
often separated from things like

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:22.560
politics or ethnicity, but this example
shows us that the world of religions

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.549
are a lot more complex than that and
that these divisions aren't usually all

00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:30.869
that clear at all. The name "Yazidi" is
contested and there's no consensus on

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:34.740
where it came from or why it was applied
to this group in particular. Some

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:39.030
connected to the middle Persian word
"Yazad" which means "divine being'', others

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:43.140
think its origins may have been the name
of the second Umayyad caliph Yazid Ibn Mu'awiya

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:47.369
whom the group that became the
Yazidis are said to have sympathized

00:01:47.369 --> 00:01:52.079
with. Yazidism has for the majority of
history and even still today had been a

00:01:52.079 --> 00:01:56.759
primarily oral tradition. This means that
there are no sacred texts or canonical

00:01:56.759 --> 00:02:00.869
collections of texts in which their
beliefs or practices are gathered or

00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:05.670
have a direct source. Instead,
beliefs and practices have been orally

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:09.390
transmitted by the religious leaders
throughout the centuries. This also means

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:12.870
that there are many different, often
competing, understandings of basic

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:16.140
tenets and elements of the religion and a huge

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:21.420
variety in how its practitioners
understand and often relate to it. This

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:26.280
of course is true for all religions but
particularly so for Yazidism because of

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.420
its very strong oral tradition and the
lack of any texts at all. There

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:35.640
is no proclamation of faith that Yazidis
affirm and no unity in terms of

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:40.260
performing rights or prayers or how
these are to be performed. This means

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:44.880
that it's hard to talk about the Yazidis
in any kind of generalizing terms, but

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.570
there are a few generalization is that we can make about

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:53.880
the general ideas or theological
tendencies and and practices of this

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.560
religion. Yazidism is usually described as
a monotheistic religion. This means that

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.800
they believe in one Almighty God who has
many names including Xwede and Allah.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:08.310
Arguably then, this is the same God as
the Abrahamic faiths as Yazidism

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:13.739
share many of the mythological basics
of these religions, which we will see. But

00:03:13.739 --> 00:03:18.900
this one God is so transcendent that he is uninterested in earthly or

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.950
worldly affairs.
Instead he focuses on "heavenly affairs"

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:27.780
and leaves the ruling or governing of the world to seven angels.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:33.269
These angels are called the "Haft Sur"
or the "Heftad". The most powerful and

00:03:33.269 --> 00:03:38.459
important of these angels is called
Melek Taus - "the peacock angel". He is the

00:03:38.459 --> 00:03:42.780
leader of the seven angels and have been
given the responsibility to do God's

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:47.519
will on earth. Melek Taus or the peacock
angel has thus become one of the most

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:52.950
prominent and important symbols for Yazidism as you can see on many logos or

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:56.790
in symbols to represent this religion.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:00.780
Another prominent symbol used by Yazidis is the
Sun which represents God and his

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:06.900
creative power, and as a result of this
Yazidis pray facing the Sun.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:11.180
These angels are also thought to be able to take
human form almost like an avatar in the

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:15.000
Hindu tradition, and have done so
throughout history. These incarnations

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.729
are known as "khass". As an example, some
Yazidis believe that Jesus and the early

00:04:20.729 --> 00:04:26.940
Muslim theologian and mystic Hasan
al-basri are two examples of this "khass".

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:28.880
Yazidis also believe in the process of

00:04:28.889 --> 00:04:33.539
reincarnation after death rather than
the more common monotheistic concept

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:37.710
of heaven and hell. The origins of the
Yazidi religion is another point of

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:42.919
contention where there really isn't any
consensus. They share the mythological

00:04:42.919 --> 00:04:48.060
background stories of the Abrahamic
faiths, and they trace their religion to

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:52.740
Adam, the first man. In fact, they often
hold that their people are descendant

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:57.720
from a miraculously born son of Adam
called Shehid Ibn Jerr, and from that

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:02.490
point on the Yazidi religion is often
traced through Babylonian, Sumerian and

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:06.990
Mithraic cultures. What is perhaps most
important to Yazidis is that they

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:11.099
consider their religion to be the original religion of

00:05:11.099 --> 00:05:16.590
the Kurdish people and that all Kurds
before the coming of Islam were Yazidis.

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:22.020
Thus, there's a clear line often drawn
here between Yazidism and Islam which

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:26.699
one should accept in one sense, but that
division or strong division also becomes

00:05:26.699 --> 00:05:31.020
slightly problematic when we view the
more scholarly historical evidence. In

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:34.860
fact, Yazidism enters the world
stage in the 12th century and

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:39.630
particularly in the region of Lalish,
which is still a central region

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:44.400
for the religion today. Here, they seem to
have first appeared as followers of a

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.320
Sufi Sheikh by the name Adi Ibn Musafir, more commonly known simply as

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:53.099
Sheikh Adi. He seems to have come originally
from modern-day Lebanon and later

00:05:53.099 --> 00:05:57.630
studied in Baghdad under, or along with,
some of the most famous Sufi scholars in

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:02.099
history, like Abd al-Qadir al-Jilani - founder
of the Qadiriyya order - and the two

00:06:02.099 --> 00:06:04.500
al-Ghazali brothers Ahmad and Abu Hamid.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.909
It seems that later in his life he chose
to live a life of seclusion and

00:06:08.909 --> 00:06:12.840
re-settled in Lalisg in the Kurdish
mountains where he founded a Sufi order

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:17.460
that he called the Adawiyya. This Sufi
order, many scholars argue,

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:22.289
is the foundation that would become
Yazidism later on. It was very

00:06:22.289 --> 00:06:26.729
characteristic of Sufi sheiks and
missionaries in particular to be more

00:06:26.729 --> 00:06:31.979
let's say intolerant or open towards
local traditions in regions that they

00:06:31.979 --> 00:06:39.210
went into. So they were often, in
their practice, adopt local practices of

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:42.719
the older religion or
tradition and sort of give it a new

00:06:42.719 --> 00:06:49.050
Islamic clothing to easily
transition the population to a new

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:55.530
Muslim identity. This fact, along with the
geographical isolation of the region of

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:59.819
Lalish and its people (and the Sufi
order) led to its gradual development

00:06:59.819 --> 00:07:06.599
away from it's Sufi or Islamic
roots into its completely own tradition

00:07:06.599 --> 00:07:11.490
eventually. Both Yazidis themselves and
modern historians and scholars agree on

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:15.629
the importance of Sheikh Adi for the
religion of Yazidism, but they have

00:07:15.629 --> 00:07:19.560
different perspectives on who he was.
Yazidis is often say that he was a

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:24.300
reformer of the already
existing Yazidi religion, and there are

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:27.029
different opinions on whether this was a
good or a bad thing.

00:07:27.029 --> 00:07:32.430
Those in favor him often claim that he was never actually a Muslim

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:36.689
but really a Yazidi in disguise who only
claimed to be a Muslim to avoid being

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:41.430
persecuted by the Sunni majority.
Scholars and historians however agree

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:46.860
that in Shaikh Adi's writings we find no
trace of Yazidism and that by all

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:52.080
accounts he seems to have been an
Orthodox Sufi Muslim who

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:57.800
created a sufi order that was within the
fold of the mainstream Islamic

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:03.719
religion, and that the deviation
or separation into it's own religion

00:08:03.719 --> 00:08:09.000
eventually happened or developed
after his death. These possible roots in

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:13.409
Sufism can also sometimes be very
clearly seen, for example, in terminology

00:08:13.409 --> 00:08:17.039
used in the religious practice. Yazidism and it's people are very much

00:08:17.039 --> 00:08:20.729
divided into a kind of caste system and
in this caste system there are a number

00:08:20.729 --> 00:08:24.900
of castes, obviously. The lower caste of
the majority are known as "Murids".

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:30.439
Above them are the Pirs or Elders who
function as spiritual guides and

00:08:30.439 --> 00:08:35.039
religious clergy basically. And lastly
the highest caste which are the

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:40.380
Sheikhs or masters who seem to have had a
rather similar role to the Pirs but

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:44.640
even more exalted. They are sometimes
thought to have healing powers for

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.990
example. For those of you who know your
Sufism or Sufi terminology you'll

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:54.010
recognize these terms, as they are the
same words that are used in the hierarchy

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:59.260
of the Sufi orders, or in the Sufi
practice generally. Murids in Sufism

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:05.440
denotes a student of a master, also known
as a Sheikh. Pir is also often used as a

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:09.970
name for the leader of a
certain Sufi order. In any case, these

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:14.650
castes have played and still do play an
important role in Yazidi practice. Like

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:19.240
with many other religions, Yazidism can
be characterized primarily as a religion

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:24.520
of orthopraxy rather than orthodoxy. This
means that they put more emphasis on

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:29.410
cultural and traditional
practices and on taboo and things

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:33.490
like that rather than having certain
beliefs or following a theological

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:38.550
doctrine. The caste system and the ethnic
unity of the Yazidi people is very

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:43.240
important. This means that one is very
rarely allowed to marry outside of the

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:47.980
religion and usually not allowed to
marry across castes either. This is in

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:51.580
fact even more frowned upon than
marrying outside of the religion all

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:54.940
together. So the role of Murid or
Shaykh is hereditary.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:59.470
If one marries outside the religion, that
person is usually excommunicated and no

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:03.490
longer considered a Yazidi. The actual
practice of the religion is often

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:09.220
connected to these ideas of taboo and
different traditional cultural practices.

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:13.030
There are festivals celebrated around
the year, like the Yazidi new year which

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:16.780
is celebrated in April. But the most
important festival of the year is

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:21.400
probably in the one known as Jamiyya or the
feast of assembly (the festival of

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:27.010
assembly) which take place over the
course of seven days. And this festival

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:31.900
is celebrated in honor of
Sheikh Adi, and during this festival Yazidis

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:37.150
make a pilgrimage to Sheikh Adi's grave
in Lalish and there they perform

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:40.810
religious rites, they listen to Qawl
(religious hymns) and stories, and

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:45.510
generally just get together as a
community.

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:02.940
Yazidis also traditionally do personal
daily prayers which are said to be five.

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:09.450
As with other minority groups the
Yazidis have in a lot of periods received less than

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:14.040
ideal treatment. They have for a large
portion of history been under the rule

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:19.920
of Muslim empires and kingdoms, and this
on-and-off persecution/oppression by the

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:24.330
Muslims can be traced, as in so many
other cases, to social and political

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:29.070
factors. So during the Ottoman era, for
example, the Yazidis were sometimes

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:35.070
tolerated, usually when they could be
beneficial to the Ottoman state and the

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:40.290
Sultan's by serving in the army or
things like this. But in other periods

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:45.150
they were often very much oppressed and
persecuted for, according to the ottomans,

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:50.880
not paying their taxes and refusing to
serve in the army. And of course there is

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:55.320
also religious grounds for much of this persecution as

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:59.970
many christians and muslims in history
have referred to as Yazidis as devil

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:04.950
worshipers. This is related to the story
of the highest angel that i mentioned

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:10.680
called Melek Taus. According to Yazidis,
Melek Taus was asked by a God to bow to

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:16.950
Adam, the first man, to which he refused
because he didn't want to bow to anyone

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:22.710
except God himself. So this was done out
of love for God. And because of this he

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:28.020
was exalted as the highest of the angels,
he was given the highest position. Now, of

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:32.160
course this is similar, or almost
identical, to the story of Iblis or

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:37.230
Satan in the Quran and the biblical
account. He was also asked to bow to Adam

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:42.090
to which he refused but in that story
God then punishes him for this refusal

00:12:42.090 --> 00:12:48.030
to follow God's orders. So this is
why some Christians and Muslims have

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:55.680
identified the the Melek Taus of the
Yazidis with the devil or Lucifer of the

00:12:55.680 --> 00:13:01.540
Abrahamic faiths. This claim the Yazidis of course
deny, but being called devil worshipers

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:06.040
doesn't exactly help your reputation and
has often led to violence or

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:10.990
justification for violence such as with
the Islamic state in 2014. But as a

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:15.160
result of things like this the Yazidis
were often not considered the among the

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:19.930
"People of the book", the Ahl al-Kitab, by the muslims and thus didn't retain the same

00:13:19.930 --> 00:13:26.170
rights as groups like christians or jews
or sometimes Zoroastrians and so on. By

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:29.440
the end of the Ottoman era the Yazidis
migrated from the Middle East to a large

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:34.390
extent and after the events of 2014
migration has become even more prominent.

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:38.080
It's hard to know how many Yazidis there are
currently in the world, estimates can

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:42.760
range between 200,000 to over a million.
The large population that previously

00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:48.010
lived in the Sinjar mountains was
severely minimized in 2014 with the

00:13:48.010 --> 00:13:52.420
genocide as 80 percent fled to other
regions or other parts of the world

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:56.890
completely. Yazidism therefore has a
significant diaspora community in Europe

00:13:56.890 --> 00:14:00.580
today. This new situation of entering
regions of the world that they

00:14:00.580 --> 00:14:04.510
historically haven't and living as a
diaspora community has led to, as it

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:08.650
often does, questions regarding
integration and the levels to which one

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:13.750
allows the religion to be adapted to the
new societies. A prime example of this is

00:14:13.750 --> 00:14:17.200
the fact that Yazidis traditionally
haven't been allowed to learn how to

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:21.130
read or write except those in the
highest (and smallest)

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:25.900
Sheikh caste. Reasons for this doctrine
isn't all that clear. Some say was meant

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:30.490
to keep the religious teachings secret
and confined to the Sheikh lineage, the

00:14:30.490 --> 00:14:36.220
religious clergy. Others say was
to avoid being educated and Islamic

00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:40.420
educational systems and schools.
Nonetheless, as the Yazidis entered

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:44.260
new regions like Europe it could become
quite a problem if you didn't know how

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:48.970
to read or write, so these  ideas have been

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:54.310
loosened up and since the 1950s the
Yazidis, especially in diaspora communities,

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:58.570
have tended to be allowed to read and
write. And this is just one example of

00:14:58.570 --> 00:15:04.960
other forms of discussions and
debates about levels of compromise or

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:10.630
integration that is allowed to modify
religious traditions. So as you can see,

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:14.890
Yazidism is a very diverse
and complex ethno-religious community

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:19.959
that is very hard to define in a lot of
cases. It is clear that even if it does

00:15:19.959 --> 00:15:25.570
have its origins in a Sufi order (the Adawiyya of Sheikh Adi) as many claim, it has

00:15:25.570 --> 00:15:29.230
throughout history developed into its
own religion and its own unique

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:33.970
tradition completely that is separated
from the religion of Islam. Thus, it can

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:39.370
tell us a lot about the myriad and vast
array of different religious groups and

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:46.080
minorities that exist in the Middle East
today. I'll see you next time.

